WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.267
-Underneath all of this

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:05.567
painful stuff and disappointment,

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:09.767
at the core, it's all about unconditional love.

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.434
♪♪

00:00:12.434 --> 00:00:15.167
I find now that I am much more forgiving

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:18.234
in my looking back at all of this.

00:00:18.234 --> 00:00:20.334
A lot is much clearer to me now.

00:00:20.334 --> 00:00:21.701
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:23.601
We're here with the wonderful Amy Poehler, everybody!

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:25.400
-[ Cheers and applause ] -Alright.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.501
So that was a clip from this wonderful

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:31.367
new documentary you directed, "Lucy and Desi."

00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:33.767
This is the first time you've directed a documentary.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.868
And what a fantastic subject to do -- Lucy and Desi.

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.200
-Yes. It was. It's been really great.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.601
It's been great to spend,

00:00:39.601 --> 00:00:41.534
you know, most of the pandemic with these people.

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:43.434
Lucy and Desi were these incredible innovators,

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:45.067
and they have a beautiful love story.

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:47.834
And we tried to tell kind of

00:00:47.834 --> 00:00:51.167
the long and everlasting ways in which they were partners,

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:52.734
in and out of each other's lives,

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:55.267
as well as the incredible work that they did.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:58.367
So it was great to be able to watch their stuff,

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.434
work on that while we were all shut down.

00:01:00.434 --> 00:01:02.200
It was awesome, and you know a lot about documentaries

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.534
because you have "Documentary Now!"

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:05.534
-I do. I do. I make fake documentaries.

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:07.467
You make the real deal. -Yeah.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:10.300
-But we've always -- You and I have always loved documentaries.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.100
-I mean, I have such high regard for the form,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.667
and I learned a ton in making this,

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:16.601
and I want to make more, and I just --

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:19.267
Yeah, I have a lot of respect. -One question I have --

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.067
Because it did seem like a lot of people

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:23.868
have always gone back and rewatched "I Love Lucy,"

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:25.434
and the pandemic really hammered home

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:28.701
how a lot of people want to return to comedy.

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:30.133
Why is that? -Yes.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:31.801
Well, another study I read...

00:01:31.801 --> 00:01:34.234
-You read a lot of studies. -I do. I do.

00:01:34.234 --> 00:01:36.701
-Yeah. Did this study give you

00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:38.000
a number at the end of it?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.767
-Yeah, at the --

00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:41.801
And it was 100. I got 100.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.734
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:47.501
That, you know, when you're going through an anxious time,

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:48.767
you like to watch things

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.234
where you know how they're going to end.

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:52.767
That's why we returned to comedy during the pandemic.

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.534
It's why we like to watch things over and over again.

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:57.434
It's why we fall asleep to shows that we love.

00:01:57.434 --> 00:02:00.100
And so there's this warmth that happens

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.801
when you're watching stuff over and over.

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:03.834
Because, like, we don't know how this --

00:02:03.834 --> 00:02:06.234
We don't know how this interview is going to end.

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:08.300
-No, we don't. -It could be a disaster.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.534
-Right. We don't know.

00:02:09.534 --> 00:02:11.534
I mean, it's going fine right now,

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:14.467
but we have had some stuff take left turns.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.634
-This desk could explode.

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:18.667
The floor could fall out from under us.

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.400
I could -- I could stomp out of here.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.701
-Yeah.

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:24.734
-So there's anxiety in that.

00:02:24.734 --> 00:02:25.968
And whereas...

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:28.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.133
But with -- with -- Yeah. With shows like --

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:31.734
You know, it's the kind of show

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:33.567
that you remember watching when you were sick.

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:35.434
-Yeah. 100%. -Right?

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:38.400
-I have a weird relationship with it because of that.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.701
I never watched it in, like, the full health,

00:02:41.701 --> 00:02:44.634
the full bloom of health. -Well, we -- It's like a show

00:02:44.634 --> 00:02:46.734
that always felt like it came with your TV.

00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:48.334
Like, it was always on. -Yeah.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.934
-But, you know, as you'll see in the doc, like, there's --

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:54.067
They have a really long career that lasts long after that show.

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.901
And, yeah. I mean, they're legends.

00:02:56.901 --> 00:02:59.934
-So he -- An interesting distinction

00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:01.701
is made between their performance styles.

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:03.267
She comes at it very much as an actor.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:07.968
He comes at it from a musician, which has a far looser style,

00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:10.167
not just in performance but in prep.

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:11.634
-Yeah. -Do you see --

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:13.200
Who did you see yourself in with that?

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.834
-Well, I think I saw myself

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.267
a little bit more in the Desi world.

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.434
But it made me -- Do you --

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:19.934
You like to rehearse a lot, right?

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:21.400
-Not anymore. -Oh.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.634
-So, the weird thing about the pandemic is, I stopped --

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:24.868
We didn't have time to rehearse.

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:27.267
-You stopped trying. -I stopped trying.

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:29.534
I sort of looked out at the world and I said,

00:03:29.534 --> 00:03:30.868
"Nobody else is trying."

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:32.701
-You were like, "No more suits!"

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:34.334
-"No more suits!

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:38.267
I'm not putting a tie on! This isn't a job interview!"

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:39.601
-Ooh. -But then --

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:41.000
So we had to move everything up.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.200
And then when we came back, we used to do a little rehearsal

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.434
in front of, like, 40 or so people that we'd find.

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:46.467
And I -- We stopped doing it,

00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:47.634
and I like doing the show a lot more.

00:03:47.634 --> 00:03:48.934
-You like it better? I know what you mean.

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:50.067
-I like finding out in the moment

00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:51.334
because it does feel a little bit more

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:53.234
like the sort of improv shows we used to do,

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:54.601
where you didn't know in advance.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.467
-Yeah, but we worked with a bunch of amazing people

00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:59.033
who had different processes of how to work.

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:01.701
Processes? "Prah-seh-sees"? "Pro-sigh"?

00:04:01.701 --> 00:04:03.000
-We can find out.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.501
I have a lot of helpful ass [bleep]

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:08.334
who watch the show that will let us know.

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:10.767
-Helpful ass [bleep], can you tell us what we did wrong?

00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:12.701
And, like, I'm thinking about Forte

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:14.133
and I'm thinking about Parnell.

00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:15.801
Chris Parnell used to rehearse and rehearse.

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:18.200
And he'd be -- We had dressing rooms next to each other,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.334
and he'd always be going over his lines.

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:21.701
And I remember being like,

00:04:21.701 --> 00:04:25.033
"Oh, I should go over my lines. Anyway..."

00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:28.033
But, yeah, so they came at it very differently.

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:30.801
People think that Lucy was this really loose, like, natural,

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:32.667
but she worked really, really hard,

00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:34.834
especially when she did so much physical stuff.

00:04:34.834 --> 00:04:39.367
-Well, it's -- I used to play -- listen on --

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.067
When we were at "SNL,"

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.634
a Walkman of people I had to do impressions of.

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:46.000
I would listen to tapes of them over and over again,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.400
and I feel like I was the most wooden impressionist.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.434
And now, I don't know if you've been watching the show,

00:04:51.434 --> 00:04:53.567
I'm incredible at impressions.

00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:55.033
-[ Laughter ] -You really are.

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:56.334
-I mean, I have just come -- And I put no thought into it.

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:57.400
-You let go. You let go.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.901
-I just say to them, "You come to me.

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:02.601
You come. You speak through me."

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.300
-No! -You don't understand this?

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.601
You're an 8.

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:07.000
A 3 would understand what I'm saying right now.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.467
-Oh, my God. Disgusting. -You come in.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.567
And then I speak -- They speak through me.

00:05:10.567 --> 00:05:11.734
-Ew. You invite them.

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:13.400
-You're making a face I don't...

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.734
-Who do you think you're good at in impressions, for real?

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:17.934
-Okay.

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.167
-You think you're good at Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:20.667
-No, I don't. I'm gonna do it. Ready?

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.467
I think I do a really good Vince Vaughn. Ready?

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:23.801
-Oh. -Okay. Ready?

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:25.100
-Yeah, I'll close my eyes.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.601
-[As Vince Vaughn] Buddy, buddy, buddy.

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:29.234
-I would never in a million years

00:05:29.234 --> 00:05:30.868
think that that was Vince Vaughn.

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:33.634
-Okay. -Not -- That's it?!

00:05:33.634 --> 00:05:35.501
-That was it, yeah. -Okay.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.834
-Okay, so, here's my Al Pacino.

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:40.367
-Oh, okay. I love Al Pacino. What a great impression.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:41.634
Let's hear it.

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:44.133
-Okay. Wait. I need to set it up.

00:05:44.133 --> 00:05:45.934
-Oh, no. That's not a good sign.

00:05:45.934 --> 00:05:47.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:48.467
"I need to set..."

00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:52.067
Do you need set it up in, "I'm about to do Al Pacino"?

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:53.968
-No, I already have done that.

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:55.767
He was in an awards show, and he was in --

00:05:55.767 --> 00:05:57.467
It was, like, one of those awards shows

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:59.501
where people were in Zoom rooms. -Yeah.

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:01.534
-So that's --

00:06:01.534 --> 00:06:04.567
Because it's a perfect phrase for Al Pacino.

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:05.934
Ready? -Okay, okay.

00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:07.567
-[As Al Pacino] I'm in the Zoom room!

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:08.934
-That's good. -That was better, right?

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:11.033
-That was good. "Buddy, buddy, buddy."

00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:13.167
That's good. That was great, Seth.

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:15.167
It was like he was here.

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:17.334
-Hey. You know what?

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:20.100
I don't think we got the gold or the silver.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.801
-We didn't, but we got the bronze!

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.634
-We got the bronze! -We got the bronze tonight!

00:06:23.634 --> 00:06:25.834
-Amy Poehler, everybody. -On the podium!

00:06:25.834 --> 00:06:28.100
-"Lucy and Desi" begins streaming tomorrow

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.968
on Amazon Prime Video.

